Business Rules:

BR-014: First name and last name are mandatory. Rationale: Legal identification requirement for maritime documentation.

BR-015: Passport number is mandatory and unique. Rationale: Primary identification document for international seafarers. Required for port state control and immigration.

BR-016: Passport expiry date must be in the future at the time of registration. Rationale: Seafarers cannot be deployed with expired travel documents.

BR-017: Nationality is mandatory, recorded as ISO 3166-1 alpha-3. Rationale: Determines applicable labor conventions and visa requirements.

BR-018: Seaman book number, if provided, must be unique. Rationale: The seaman book is an official maritime document that is individually assigned.

BR-019: Seaman book expiry date, if provided, must be in the future. Rationale: Same logic as passport — expired documents prevent deployment.

BR-020: Rank is mandatory. Rationale: Determines what positions the seafarer can fill on a vessel. Critical for manning compliance.

BR-021: Date of birth, if provided, must be in the past. Rationale: Basic data integrity.

BR-022: Search by passport number is always exact match. Rationale: Passport numbers are precise identifiers — same reasoning as IMO number search.

BR-023: Rank changes must be tracked. For now, the current rank is simply overwritten. When we build promotion history (future epic), this will become a more formal process.
Rationale: Documenting this decision so we know the limitation exists.

BR-024: Deactivating a seafarer does not affect historical records (future voyage/assignment data). Rationale: Same as vessel deactivation — past data stays valid.